BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:VCEE Brownbag-Seminar - Chris Hoehne
DTSTART:20251124T121500Z
DTEND:20251124T131500Z
DTSTAMP:20251202T212450Z
UID:4hq4mh8691r6g2ait2nrmh9gcn@google.com
SEQUENCE:1
CREATED:20251008T085356Z
DESCRIPTION:<b><u>Speaker:</u></b> Chris Hoehne<br><br><b><u>Title: </u>
 </b> Polarization based on Moralisation: An Experimental Design Proposal<
 br><br><br><u><b>Abstract: </b></u><br>Polarization has gained widespread
  attention. Yet\, the underlying mechanism for the (perceived) increase in
  polarization isn’t well understood. In this project\, I aim to examine 
 morals and moral narratives as a potential driver\, as public discourse se
 ems to increasingly hinge on normative judgments of what is “right” or
  “wrong”. If people internalise these moralized narratives and convert
  them into value judgments of other people’s character\, disagreement ma
 y escalate into animosity. I will present an experimental design to test
  this potential link between morals and affective polarization. Specifical
 ly\, I aim to distinguish whether people punish others based on the action
  they observe or on the moral justification attached to that action. <br>
 <br>*Pizza at 13:00*
LAST-MODIFIED:20251120T122331Z
LOCATION:OMDS Seminar Room (#3.307)\, OMP-1
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:VCEE Brownbag - Seminar - Ada Kovaliukaite
DTSTART:20251110T121500Z
DTEND:20251110T131500Z
DTSTAMP:20251202T212450Z
UID:6a8e396lo94e69t8vnj4509o67@google.com
SEQUENCE:1
CREATED:20251008T085300Z
DESCRIPTION:<b><u>Speaker:</u></b> Ada Kovaliukaite<br><br><b><u>Title:</u
 ></b> Resisting Policy Advice<br><br><b><u>Abstract:</u></b> We study how
  individuals adopt or resist policy recommendations in a policy lab combin
 ing a controlled laboratory experiment with an online experiment on a repr
 esentative U.S. sample. In the lab\, participants compete in a Tullock con
 test according to two competition rules—an equal‐opportunity rule and
  an asymmetric rule—and we experimentally vary which rule is framed as l
 egitimate. This establishes common knowledge about outcomes while manipula
 ting only the value interpretation of the policy. In the online experiment
 \, impartial spectators choose between the same two rules to be implemente
 d in the future laboratory experiments. We find that voter support for the
  rules varies based on the legitimacy framing and is resistant to informat
 ion. Exposure to politically slanted messaging impacts policy support and 
 the effects are different for informed and uninformed agents. Finally\, we
  investigate the heterogeneous slant effects depending on individual polit
 ical ideology\, prior beliefs\, distributive preferences\, and demographic
  characteristics.<br><br><br><b>Pizza at 13:00</b>
LAST-MODIFIED:20251109T205141Z
LOCATION:OMDS Seminar Room (#3.307)\, OMP-1
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
